For 8 items and 8 properties, brute force is genuinely fine — no need for anything clever. The one refinement worth making: don't check all 8 properties blindly, since missing values are common. Compare on the union of keys that actually exist.

```javascript
function sameObject(a, b) {
  const keys = new Set([...Object.keys(a), ...Object.keys(b)]);
  for (const k of keys) {
    if (a[k] !== b[k]) return false;
  }
  return true;
}

function sameArray(a, b) {
  return a.length === b.length && a.every((o, i) => sameObject(o, b[i]));
}
```

This treats `{x: 1}` and `{x: 1, y: undefined}` as equal, which is usually what you want with sparse objects. If you need order-independence, sort both arrays by a stable key first. If values can be nested objects or arrays, `!==` won't work — you'd need a deep-equal, and at that point `JSON.stringify` with sorted keys is a reasonable shortcut for this size.

